@end menu

@node Lexical Elements
@section Lexical Elements

The AGI frontend lexer tokenizes input into the following categories:

@table @asis
@item Keywords
Reserved words that have special meaning in the language. All keywords are
case-insensitive and conventionally written in UPPERCASE.
@item Operators
Symbols representing arithmetic, logical, and comparison operations.
@item Delimiters
Punctuation used for grouping and separation.
@item Literals
Numeric and string constants.
@item Identifiers
User-defined names for variables, routines, and labels.
@end table

@subsection Keywords

The following keywords are recognized by the AGI frontend lexer (case-insensitive):

@table @asis
@item @code{IF}
Starts a conditional statement. See @ref{Control Flow}.
@item @code{ELSE}
Specifies the false branch of an IF statement.
@item @code{THEN}
Separates the condition from the true branch in an IF statement.
@item @code{END-IF}
Optional marker for the end of an IF block (for readability; not required by parser).
@item @code{GOSUB}
Calls a subroutine. See @ref{Subroutine Call}.
@item @code{GOTO}
Jumps to a labeled statement. See @ref{Unconditional Jump}.
@item @code{RETURN}
Returns from a subroutine. See @ref{Subroutine Return}.
@item @code{INCREMENT}
Increments a variable by 1. See @ref{Increment}.
@item @code{DECREMENT}
Decrements a variable by 1. See @ref{Decrement}.
@item @code{SET}
Sets a variable to 1 (boolean true). See @ref{Set to One}.
@item @code{RESET}
Sets a variable to 0 (boolean false). See @ref{Reset to Zero}.
@item @code{ASSIGN}
Assignment operator (reserved; use @code{=} instead). See @ref{Assignment}.
@item @code{PRINT}
Display text on screen. See @ref{Print Statement}.
@item @code{INPUT}
Get user input. See @ref{Input Statement}.
@item @code{DIALOGUE}
Display dialogue with speaker name. See @ref{Dialogue Statement}.
@item @code{AND}
Logical conjunction operator for combining conditions.
@item @code{OR}
Logical disjunction operator for combining conditions.
@item @code{NOT}
Logical negation operator for inverting conditions.
@end table

@subsection Operators

The following operators are supported:

@table @asis
@item @code{+}
Addition. Combines two numeric values.
@item @code{-}
Subtraction. Subtracts right operand from left.
@item @code{*}
Multiplication. Multiplies two numeric values.
@item @code{/}
Division. Divides left operand by right.
@item @code{=}
Assignment (sets variable to value) and equality comparison (tests equality).
@item @code{<}
Less than comparison. Returns 1 if true, 0 if false.
@item @code{>}
Greater than comparison. Returns 1 if true, 0 if false.
@item @code{<=}
Less than or equal comparison.
@item @code{>=}
Greater than or equal comparison.
@item @code{<>}
Not equal comparison. Returns 1 if operands differ.
@item @code{<<}
Left bit shift. Shifts bits left by specified amount.
@item @code{>>}
Right bit shift. Shifts bits right by specified amount.
@item @code{.}
Reserved for future use; not implemented in the parser.
@end table

@subsection Delimiters

The following delimiters are used:

@table @asis
@item @code{(}
Left parenthesis for grouping expressions.
@item @code{)}
Right parenthesis for grouping expressions.
@item @code{,}
Comma for separating items (reserved for future use).
@item @code{:}
Colon for separating items (reserved for future use).
@end table

@subsection Identifiers

Identifiers consist of letters, digits, underscores (@code{_}), and hyphens (@code{-}).
They must begin with a letter or underscore. Identifiers are case-insensitive and
normalized to @code{Header.Case} format in the AST.

See @ref{Identifier Normalization} for details on the normalization process.

@node Numeric Literals
@section Numeric Literals

The AGI frontend supports multiple numeric literal formats for convenience:

@table @asis
@item Decimal
Bare digits (@code{42}, @code{0}, @code{123}) or @code{d'@var{digits}'} format.
@*
Examples: @code{100}, @code{d'255'}, @code{d'0'}

@item Hexadecimal
@code{x'@var{hex-digits}'} format with optional commas for readability.
@*
Examples: @code{x'FF'} (255), @code{x'1A'} (26), @code{x'1A,2B'} (6682)

@item Octal
@code{o'@var{octal-digits}'} format.
@*
Examples: @code{o'777'} (511), @code{o'42'} (34)

@item Binary
@code{b'@var{binary-digits}'} format.
@*
Examples: @code{b'1010'} (10), @code{b'11111111'} (255)

@item Word/Dword (String)
@code{w'@var{data}'} or @code{w"@var{data}"} format. Returns the string data
as-is for backend processing.
@*
Examples: @code{w'ABCD'}, @code{w"test"}
@end table

All numeric formats (except word/dword strings) are internally converted to their
decimal integer value for AST representation. This allows uniform handling
regardless of input format.

@node Statements
@section Statements

An AGI program is a sequence of statements, each executed in order unless modified
by control flow statements. Statements may be separated by newlines or placed on
the same line (parser processes sequentially).

@menu
* Assignment Statement::
* Increment and Decrement::
* Set and Reset::
* Print Statement::
* Input Statement::
* Dialogue Statement::
@end menu

@node Assignment Statement
@subsection Assignment Statement

@defsyn
@var{variable} = @var{expression}
@end defsyn

Evaluates @var{expression} and stores the result in @var{variable}.
The @code{=} operator performs both assignment and equality testing depending on context.

@subsubsection Syntax Variations

@table @asis
@item Basic assignment
@code{@var{variable} = @var{expression}}
@*
Assigns the result of expression to variable.

@item Assignment with parentheses
@code{(@var{variable} = @var{expression})}
@*
Assignment within a condition.
@end table

@subsubsection Examples

@example
player_score = 0
x = (y + 10)
score = (score + points)
flag = (status = 1)
@end example

@subsubsection Valid Ranges

Variables store integer values. The valid range depends on the backend target:
@itemize @bullet
@item 8-bit targets: 0-255 (unsigned) or -128 to 127 (signed)
@item 16-bit targets: 0-65535 (unsigned) or -32768 to 32767 (signed)
@item Larger ranges available for multi-byte storage
@end itemize

@node Increment and Decrement
@subsection Increment and Decrement

@subsubsection Increment

@defsyn
INCREMENT @var{variable}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
INCREMENT (@var{variable})
@end defsyn

Adds 1 to @var{variable}. Equivalent to @code{@var{variable} = @var{variable} + 1}.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
INCREMENT lives
INCREMENT (counter)
@end example

@subsubsection Decrement

@defsyn
DECREMENT @var{variable}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
DECREMENT (@var{variable})
@end defsyn

Subtracts 1 from @var{variable}. Equivalent to @code{@var{variable} = @var{variable} - 1}.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
DECREMENT lives
DECREMENT (health)
@end example

@subsubsection Notes

Both INCREMENT and DECREMENT operate atomically and are useful for counters,
loop variables, and game state tracking.

@node Set and Reset
@subsection Set and Reset

@subsubsection Set to One

@defsyn
SET @var{variable}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
SET (@var{variable}, @var{expression})
@end defsyn

Sets @var{variable} to 1 (boolean true) in the simple form.
In the parenthesized form, sets the variable to the result of expression.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
SET game_started
SET (difficulty, 5)
@end example

@subsubsection Reset to Zero

@defsyn
RESET @var{variable}
@end defsyn

Sets @var{variable} to 0 (boolean false). Used to clear flags and boolean states.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
RESET flag
RESET game_over
@end example

@subsubsection Notes

SET and RESET are optimized for boolean flag operations. The simple form of SET
is faster than @code{variable = 1} as it may generate more efficient code.

@node Print Statement
@subsection Print Statement

@defsyn
PRINT "@var{message}"
@end defsyn

@defsyn
PRINT "@var{message}" @var{x} @var{y}
@end defsyn

Displays text on the screen at the current cursor position (simple form) or
at coordinates (x, y) when specified.

@subsubsection Parameters

@table @asis
@item @var{message}
String literal containing the text to display. Must be enclosed in double quotes.
@item @var{x}
Horizontal position (optional). Integer or numeric expression.
@item @var{y}
Vertical position (optional). Integer or numeric expression.
@end table

@subsubsection Examples

@example
PRINT "Hello, world!"
PRINT "Score: " score
PRINT "Game Over" 10 20
@end example

@subsubsection Output

The print statement generates a :print AST node. The actual rendering depends
on the backend target (console, graphics buffer, etc.).

@node Input Statement
@subsection Input Statement

@defsyn
INPUT @var{variable}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
INPUT @var{variable} @var{max-length}
@end defsyn

Reads user input and stores the result in @var{variable}.

@subsubsection Parameters

@table @asis
@item @var{variable}
Identifier to store the input value.
@item @var{max-length}
Maximum length of input (optional). Limits how many characters are read.
@end table

@subsubsection Examples

@example
INPUT player_name
INPUT answer 50
@end example

@subsubsection Input Types

The backend determines the input type based on context:
@itemize @bullet
@item Numeric contexts expect integer input
@item String contexts allow text input
@end itemize

@node Dialogue Statement
@subsection Dialogue Statement

@defsyn
DIALOGUE "@var{speaker}" "@var{text}"
@end defsyn

Displays dialogue text attributed to a speaker, commonly used for NPC conversations
and narrative text display.

@subsubsection Parameters

@table @asis
@item @var{speaker}
String literal naming the speaking character.
@item @var{text}
String literal containing the dialogue text.
@end table

@subsubsection Examples

@example
DIALOGUE "Guard" "Halt! Who goes there?"
DIALOGUE "Narrator" "You enter a dark cave."
@end example

@subsubsection Output

Generates a :dialogue AST node that the backend renders appropriately for the
target platform (text adventure, graphical, etc.).

@node Control Flow
@section Control Flow

@menu
* Conditional Statement::
* Subroutine Call::
* Unconditional Jump::
* Subroutine Return::
@end menu

@node Conditional Statement
@subsection Conditional Statement: IF-THEN-ELSE

@defsyn
IF @var{condition} THEN @var{statement} [ELSE @var{statement}]
@end defsyn

Executes @var{statement} if @var{condition} evaluates to true (non-zero);
otherwise, executes the @var{statement} in the ELSE clause if present.
The END-IF keyword is optional and ignored by the parser (used for readability).

@subsubsection Syntax Variations

@table @asis
@item Simple conditional
@code{IF @var{condition} THEN @var{statement}}
@*
Executes statement if condition is true.

@item Conditional with else
@code{IF @var{condition} THEN @var{statement} ELSE @var{statement}}
@*
Executes first statement if true, second if false.

@item With END-IF marker
@code{IF @var{condition} THEN @var{statements} END-IF}
@*
END-IF is ignored but improves readability.
@end table

@subsubsection Examples

@example
IF (score > 100) THEN
    SET difficulty 1
ELSE
    SET difficulty 0
END-IF

IF (lives > 0) THEN
    INCREMENT attempts
END-IF
@end example

@subsubsection Valid Conditions

Conditions are expressions that evaluate to true (non-zero) or false (zero).
See @ref{Expressions} for expression syntax.

@node Subroutine Call
@subsection Subroutine Call: GOSUB

@defsyn
GOSUB @var{routine-name}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
GOSUB (@var{routine-name})
@end defsyn

Calls the subroutine named @var{routine-name}. Execution continues after the GOSUB
statement when a RETURN is encountered in the subroutine.

@subsubsection Syntax Variations

@table @asis
@item Without parentheses
@code{GOSUB @var{routine-name}}
@*
Direct call with routine name.

@item With parentheses
@code{GOSUB (@var{routine-name})}
@*
Parenthesized form for clarity or precedence.
@end table

@subsubsection Examples

@example
GOSUB InitializeGame
GOSUB (UpdateScore)
GOSUB ProcessInput
@end example

@subsubsection Notes

GOSUB calls are analogous to function calls in other languages. The subroutine
must contain a RETURN statement to return control to the caller.

@node Unconditional Jump
@subsection Unconditional Jump: GOTO

@defsyn
GOTO @var{label}
@end defsyn

Transfers control to the statement following the label @var{label}.
Labels are identifiers defined elsewhere in the program.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
GOTO MainLoop
GOTO GAME_OVER
GOTO Retry
@end example

@subsubsection Notes

@itemize @bullet
@item GOTO provides unstructured control flow
@item Use sparingly; prefer GOSUB/RETURN for maintainability
@item Labels must be defined in the same scope
@end itemize

@node Subroutine Return
@subsection Subroutine Return: RETURN

@defsyn
RETURN
@end defsyn

Returns from the current subroutine to the statement following the most recent GOSUB.
In a top-level program, RETURN terminates execution.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
RETURN

MyRoutine:
    SET processed 1
    RETURN
@end example

@subsubsection Notes

Each GOSUB should have a matching RETURN. Unmatched RETURNs at program level
terminate the program.

@node Arithmetic Operations
@section Arithmetic Operations

The AGI frontend supports standard arithmetic operations. See @ref{Operator Precedence}
for information on how operations are evaluated.

@menu
* Addition and Subtraction::
* Multiplication and Division::
* Bit Shifting::
@end menu

@node Addition and Subtraction
@subsection Addition and Subtraction

@defsyn
@var{expression} + @var{expression}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{expression} - @var{expression}
@end defsyn

Addition (+) produces the sum of two operands.
Subtraction (-) produces the difference (left minus right).

@subsubsection Examples

@example
score + 10
(x + y)
(score - penalty)
(total - 100)
@end example

@subsubsection Valid Ranges

Results are computed using integer arithmetic. Overflow behavior depends on the
backend target (wrap-around, saturation, or error).

@node Multiplication and Division
@subsection Multiplication and Division

@defsyn
@var{expression} * @var{expression}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{expression} / @var{expression}
@end defsyn

Multiplication (*) produces the product of two operands.
Division (/) produces the quotient (left divided by right). Integer division
truncates toward zero.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
score * 2
(width * height)
(total / 2)
(points / players)
@end example

@subsubsection Notes

@itemize @bullet
@item Division by zero produces undefined behavior
@item For multiplication by powers of 2, consider bit shifting for efficiency
@end itemize

@node Bit Shifting
@subsection Bit Shifting

@defsyn
@var{expression} << @var{expression}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{expression} >> @var{expression}
@end defsyn

Left shift (<<) moves bits left, filling with zeros. Equivalent to multiplication
by powers of 2.
Right shift (>>) moves bits right, discarding shifted bits. Equivalent to division
by powers of 2.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
value << 2          @r{; Multiply by 4}
flags << 3          @r{; Multiply by 8}
packed >> 1         @r{; Divide by 2}
shift >> 4          @r{; Divide by 16}
@end example

@subsubsection Equivalencies

@table @asis
@item @code{x << n} is equivalent to @code{x * 2^n}
@item @code{x >> n} is equivalent to @code{x / 2^n} (integer division)
@end table

@node Comparison Operations
@section Comparison Operations

Comparison operations evaluate to 1 (true) or 0 (false). They are commonly used
in IF conditions and while loops.

@menu
* Equality and Inequality::
* Relational Comparisons::
@end menu

@node Equality and Inequality
@subsection Equality and Inequality

@defsyn
@var{expression} = @var{expression}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{expression} <> @var{expression}
@end defsyn

Equality (=) returns 1 if operands are equal, 0 otherwise.
Inequality (<>) returns 1 if operands differ, 0 otherwise.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(score = 100)
(x = 0)
(flag <> 1)
(name <> "unknown")
@end example

@node Relational Comparisons
@subsection Relational Comparisons

@defsyn
@var{expression} < @var{expression}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{expression} > @var{expression}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{expression} <= @var{expression}
@end defsyn

@defsyn
@var{expression} >= @var{expression}
@end defsyn

Less than (<), greater than (>), less than or equal (<=), and greater than or
equal (>=) compare two numeric operands.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(score < 10)
(level > 5)
(health <= 0)
(age >= 18)
@end example

@node Logical Operations
@section Logical Operations

Logical operations combine boolean values and are typically used in conditions.

@menu
* Logical AND::
* Logical OR::
* Logical NOT::
@end menu

@node Logical AND
@subsection Logical AND

@defsyn
@var{condition} AND @var{condition}
@end defsyn

Returns true if both operands are true (non-zero). Evaluation is left-to-right
with short-circuit behavior (second operand not evaluated if first is false).

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(score > 0) AND (lives > 0)
(x >= 0) AND (x < 100)
(active = 1) AND (visible = 1)
@end example

@node Logical OR
@subsection Logical OR

@defsyn
@var{condition} OR @var{condition}
@end defsyn

Returns true if either operand is true (non-zero). Evaluation is left-to-right
with short-circuit behavior (second operand not evaluated if first is true).

@subsubsection Examples

@example
(score > 100) OR (bonus > 50)
(level = 1) OR (level = 2)
(end = 1) OR (quit = 1)
@end example

@node Logical NOT
@subsection Logical NOT

@defsyn
NOT @var{condition}
@end defsyn

Returns true if the operand is false (zero), and vice versa.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
NOT done
NOT (score = 0)
NOT ((x < 0) OR (x > 100))
@end example

@node Game Commands
@section Game Commands

The following commands are reserved for future implementation for game-specific
functionality:

@table @asis
@item @code{NEWROOM}
Load and transition to a new room or map. Reserved for future use.

@item @code{LOADLOGICS}
Load logic/script resources. Reserved for future use.

@item @code{QUIT}
End game and return to title screen. Reserved for future use.

@item @code{SAID}
Parse and respond to player text input. Reserved for future use.

@item @code{TEST}
Test multiple conditions. Reserved for future use.

@item @code{POSN}
Check if position is within bounding box. Reserved for future use.

@item @code{CONTROLLER}
Test controller/input state. Reserved for future use.

@item @code{HAVEKEY}
Check if keyboard input is available. Reserved for future use.
@end table

These keywords are recognized by the lexer but have no effect in the current
parser implementation.

@node Input and Output
@section Input and Output

The AGI frontend provides basic I/O commands for user interaction:

@table @asis
@item PRINT
Display text on screen (see @ref{Print Statement})
@item INPUT
Read user input (see @ref{Input Statement})
@item DIALOGUE
Display attributed dialogue (see @ref{Dialogue Statement})
@end table

@node Expressions
@section Expressions

Expressions compute values and can consist of literals, identifiers, operators,
and parentheses for grouping.

@subsubsection Primary Expressions

Primary expressions are the building blocks:

@table @asis
@item Numeric literals: @code{42}, @code{0}, @code{x'FF'}, @code{o'77'}, @code{b'1010'}
@item String literals: @code{"Hello, world!"}
@item Identifiers: @code{score}, @code{player_x}, @code{Player.Score}
@end table

@subsubsection Compound Expressions

Expressions can be combined using operators:

@example
score + 10
(x * y) / 2
value << 2
(score = 100)
(score > 0) AND (lives > 0)
@end example

@subsubsection Grouping

Parentheses control evaluation order:

@example
((a + b) * c)
(x = (y + 10))
((score > 0) AND (lives > 0))
@end example

@node Operator Precedence
@section Operator Precedence

Operators have the following precedence (highest first):

@enumerate
@item Parentheses @code{( @var{expression} @code{)}}
@item Multiplication and division: @code{*}, @code{/}
@item Bit shifts: @code{<<}, @code{>>}
@item Addition and subtraction: @code{+}, @code{-}
@item Comparisons: @code{=}, @code{<}, @code{>}, @code{<=}, @code{>=}, @code{<>}
@item Logical NOT: @code{NOT}
@item Logical AND: @code{AND}
@item Logical OR: @code{OR}
@end enumerate

When operators have equal precedence, they evaluate left-to-right except for
NOT which is right-associative.

@subsubsection Examples

@example
a + b * c           @r{; Equivalent to: a + (b * c)}
(a + b) * c         @r{; Addition before multiplication}
NOT a AND b         @r{; Equivalent to: (NOT a) AND b}
a AND b OR c        @r{; Equivalent to: (a AND b) OR c}
a OR b AND c        @r{; AND has higher precedence: a OR (b AND c)}
@end example

@node Identifier Normalization
@section Identifier Normalization

All identifiers are automatically normalized to the @code{Header.Case} format with
dots as segment separators when converted to AST nodes.

@subsubsection Normalization Rules

@table @asis
@item Input: @code{player-name}
@*
Output: @code{Player.Name}

@item Input: @code{player_score}
@*
Output: @code{Player.Score}

@item Input: @code{getItemCount}
@*
Output: @code{Get.Item.Count}

@item Input: @code{my_var_name}
@*
Output: @code{My.Var.Name}

@item Input: @code{X}
@*
Output: @code{X} (unchanged if no separators)
@end table

Hyphens and underscores are both converted to dots in the output. CamelCase
identifiers are split at case transitions.

@node Unsupported Commands
@section Unsupported Commands

The following AGI-like constructs are recognized by the lexer but not implemented
in the parser. They are reserved for future development:

@table @asis
@item @code{NEWROOM @var{room-id}}
Load and display a new room. Planned for room management.

@item @code{LOADLOGICS @var{script-id}}
Load and execute a logic/script file. Planned for modular game logic.

@item @code{QUIT}
End the game and return to the title screen. Planned for game termination.

@item @code{ASSIGN @var{variable} @var{value}}
Explicit assignment keyword. Use @code{variable = value} instead.

@item @code{SAID @var{word-list}}
Parse player input against vocabulary. Planned for text parser integration.

@item @code{TEST @var{var1} @var{var2} @dots{}}
Test multiple variables for specific patterns. Planned for testing utilities.

@item @code{POSN @var{left} @var{top} @var{right} @var{bottom} [variable]}
Check if position is within bounding box. Planned for collision detection.

@item @code{CONTROLLER @var{controller-id}}
Test if input controller is active. Planned for input handling.

@item @code{HAVEKEY}
Check if keyboard input is waiting. Planned for input buffering.
@end table

These commands are syntactically valid but produce no code generation in the
current implementation.

@node Example Programs
@section Example Programs

@subsubsection Game State Machine

@example
;; Game state initialization
SET score 0
SET lives 3
SET game_over 0

;; Main game loop
MAIN_LOOP:
IF (game_over = 1) THEN
    GOTO END_GAME
ELSE
    GOSUB UpdateGame
END-IF

IF (score > 1000) THEN
    SET lives (lives - 1)
    SET score 0
END-IF

GOTO MAIN_LOOP

;; Game over handler
END_GAME:
PRINT "Game Over!"
PRINT "Final Score: " score
RETURN
@end example

@subsubsection Inventory Management

@example
;; Initialize inventory
SET has_key 0
SET has_sword 0
SET gold 100

;; Pick up item
PICKUP:
IF (item = 1) THEN
    SET has_key 1
    PRINT "You found a key!"
ELSE
    IF (item = 2) THEN
        SET has_sword 1
        PRINT "You found a sword!"
    ELSE
        PRINT "Unknown item."
    END-IF
END-IF

RETURN
@end example

@subsubsection Dialogue System

@example
;; NPC dialogue routine
TALK_GUARD:
DIALOGUE "Guard" "Halt! Who goes there?"

INPUT response

IF (has_key = 1) THEN
    DIALOGUE "Guard" "Oh, you have the key. Pass."
    GOSUB OpenDoor
ELSE
    DIALOGUE "Guard" "You need permission to enter."
END-IF

RETURN
@end example


